      <h1 class="leading-tight mb-4">How to Buy a Vacation Home in Pombal, Portugal</h1>
      <p class="text-lg text-muted font-body">Six years ago, we arrived in Abiul with an idea. It took a while before the first stone was turned, but today Casa dos Duques exists. Here&rsquo;s the practical, no-nonsense guide to buying your own holiday home in Central Portugal &mdash; with the people we trust.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="text-muted leading-relaxed space-y-4 font-body">
      <p>You&rsquo;ve been scrolling through listings on your phone. A whitewashed house with blue trim. An old stone cottage with a fig tree in the garden. The numbers look surprisingly reasonable compared to back home. And then the thought lands: <em>Could we actually do this?</em></p>

      <p>The answer is yes. And Pombal &mdash; a working Portuguese town under two hours north of Lisbon, with a Templar castle on the hill and four UNESCO sites within 50 minutes &mdash; is one of the smartest places to do it. Property prices here haven&rsquo;t caught up with the Algarve or Lisbon. The beaches are quieter. And the town has everything you actually need: supermarkets, hospitals, restaurants, and a train station with direct connections to Lisbon and Porto.</p>

      <p>This isn&rsquo;t a theoretical guide. Six years ago we arrived in Abiul. It took time &mdash; finding the right property, navigating the financing, planning the renovation &mdash; but we bought and restored a 90-year-old house in <a href="/guide/abiul/" class="text-terracotta-500 hover:text-terracotta-600 underline">Abiul</a>, just outside Pombal, and turned it into <a href="/the-house/" class="text-terracotta-500 hover:text-terracotta-600 underline">Casa dos Duques</a> &mdash; a holiday rental that now welcomes guests from all over the world. The people we recommend below are the ones we worked with ourselves. No affiliations, no commissions &mdash; just who we&rsquo;d call if we did it again tomorrow.</p>

      <h2 class="mt-8 mb-3">Step 1: Find the right property</h2>

      <p>You can browse from home. <a href="https://www.idealista.pt" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-terracotta-500 hover:text-terracotta-600 underline">Idealista.pt</a> is Portugal&rsquo;s biggest property portal &mdash; it aggregates listings from agencies and private sellers across the country. Filter by district (Leiria), municipality (Pombal), and set your budget. You&rsquo;ll quickly get a feel for what&rsquo;s available: village houses from &euro;50,000, town apartments from &euro;80,000, and detached houses with land from &euro;150,000.</p>

      <p>But a portal can only show you so much. The best properties in rural Central Portugal often don&rsquo;t make it online &mdash; or they appear with three blurry photos and a one-line description that doesn&rsquo;t mention the olive grove out back or the fact the roof was replaced last year.</p>

      <p>This is where a local agent earns their keep. We recommend <strong><a href="https://www.habifit.pt" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-terracotta-500 hover:text-terracotta-600 underline">Habifit</a></strong>, a real estate consultancy specialising in the Pombal market. They know the area, they know which streets flood in winter and which villages have good sun exposure, and &mdash; critically &mdash; they speak Portuguese and understand the local bureaucracy. That last part matters more than you think when you&rsquo;re trying to figure out whether a property has a valid habitation licence or if that extension was ever properly registered.</p>

      <div class="bg-stone-100 p-4 mb-4 text-sm">
        <p><strong>&#127968; Habifit &mdash; Media&ccedil;&atilde;o e Consultoria Imobili&aacute;ria</strong></p>
        <p><strong>&#128205; Area:</strong> Pombal and surrounding region, with national coverage</p>
        <p><strong>&#127760; Web:</strong> <a href="https://www.habifit.pt" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-terracotta-500 hover:text-terracotta-600 underline">habifit.pt</a></p>
        <p class="text-xs text-muted mt-2">Specialist in the local Pombal market. Portuguese-speaking, paperwork-savvy, and genuinely knowledgeable about the area.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 class="mt-8 mb-3">Step 2: Sort out the financing</h2>

      <p>Unless you&rsquo;re buying in cash, you&rsquo;ll need a mortgage. And even if you are buying cash, you might still want one &mdash; Portuguese mortgage rates for non-residents are often surprisingly competitive, and keeping cash in your pocket can be smarter than locking it all into one asset.</p>

      <p>Here&rsquo;s the single best piece of advice in this article: <strong>don&rsquo;t walk into your bank and take whatever they offer.</strong> Portuguese banks vary wildly in what they&rsquo;ll lend to foreigners, at what rate, and with what conditions. One bank might lend 80% of the purchase price; another might cap you at 60%. One might accept your foreign income documents without blinking; another might ask for three years of Portuguese tax returns you don&rsquo;t have.</p>

      <p>A <strong>credit intermediary</strong> solves this. They&rsquo;re regulated by the Bank of Portugal, they compare offers across multiple banks (usually 10&ndash;12), and &mdash; here&rsquo;s the important part &mdash; <strong>their service is free to you</strong>. The bank pays them, not you.</p>

      <p>We recommend <strong><a href="https://www.financialoptions.pt" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-terracotta-500 hover:text-terracotta-600 underline">Financial Options</a></strong>, a credit intermediary registered with the Bank of Portugal. We know these people personally &mdash; they&rsquo;ve helped clients from across Europe and beyond secure mortgages for properties in the Pombal region, including ours. They understand what foreign buyers need, they know which banks play nicely with non-resident applications, and they handle the paperwork so you don&rsquo;t drown in it.</p>

      <div class="bg-stone-100 p-4 mb-4 text-sm">
        <p><strong>&#127974; Financial Options &mdash; Intermedi&aacute;rio de Cr&eacute;dito</strong></p>
        <p><strong>&#128205; Address:</strong> Rua D. Afonso Henriques n&ordm;9, Abiul 3100-012</p>
        <p><strong>&#128222; Phone:</strong> +351 964 677 002</p>
