        <li><a href="https://www.financialoptions.pt/guias/sinal-cpcv-escritura-etapas/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-terracotta-500 hover:text-terracotta-600 underline">Deposit, CPCV, and deed: the steps to buying</a> &mdash; how the Portuguese purchase process works</li>
        <li><a href="https://www.financialoptions.pt/guias/imt-imposto-selo-compra-casa/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-terracotta-500 hover:text-terracotta-600 underline">IMT and Stamp Duty when buying a house</a> &mdash; taxes explained in plain language</li>
        <li><a href="https://www.financialoptions.pt/guias/comprar-casa-para-arrendar/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-terracotta-500 hover:text-terracotta-600 underline">Buying a house to rent out</a> &mdash; what changes in the mortgage when it&rsquo;s not your primary residence</li>
        <li><a href="https://www.financialoptions.pt/guias/o-que-faz-intermediario-credito/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-terracotta-500 hover:text-terracotta-600 underline">What a credit intermediary does</a> &mdash; and why you want one</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="mt-8 mb-3">Step 3: The legal stuff (don&rsquo;t skip this)</h2>

      <p>Buying property in Portugal follows a well-established process, but it&rsquo;s different from the UK, US, or Northern Europe. The short version:</p>

      <p><strong>Get a NIF</strong> (N&uacute;mero de Identifica&ccedil;&atilde;o Fiscal) &mdash; a Portuguese tax number. You cannot buy property without one. It&rsquo;s quick and cheap (around &euro;10&ndash;15 at a Finan&ccedil;as office), but you&rsquo;ll need a fiscal representative if you&rsquo;re not an EU resident.</p>

      <p><strong>Sign the CPCV</strong> (Contrato de Promessa de Compra e Venda) &mdash; the promissory contract. This locks in the deal. You pay a deposit (usually 10&ndash;20%), and both sides commit. If the seller pulls out, they pay you double the deposit back. If you pull out, you lose the deposit. Don&rsquo;t sign one without a lawyer checking it first.</p>

      <p><strong>Complete the deed</strong> (Escritura) &mdash; the final contract, signed at a notary office. This is when you pay the remaining balance, the taxes (IMT and Stamp Duty), and the notary fees. The keys are yours.</p>

      <p>Financial Options has a detailed guide walking through <a href="https://www.financialoptions.pt/guias/sinal-cpcv-escritura-etapas/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-terracotta-500 hover:text-terracotta-600 underline">every step from deposit to deed</a>, including timelines and what to expect at each stage.</p>

      <h2 class="mt-8 mb-3">Step 4: After you have the keys</h2>

      <p>You&rsquo;ve done it. The house is yours. Now what?</p>

      <p>If you&rsquo;re planning to use it as a holiday home (and not rent it out), your main ongoing costs are <strong>IMI</strong> (annual municipal property tax, roughly 0.3&ndash;0.5% of the property&rsquo;s taxable value), utilities, and insurance.</p>

      <p>If you&rsquo;re thinking of renting it out when you&rsquo;re not there &mdash; which is what we do with <a href="/the-house/" class="text-terracotta-500 hover:text-terracotta-600 underline">Casa dos Duques</a> &mdash; you&rsquo;ll need to register the property for short-term rental (Alojamento Local), declare the income in Portugal, and budget for maintenance between guests. It&rsquo;s work, but it can cover a significant chunk of your mortgage and running costs while keeping the house lived-in and cared for.</p>

      <p>The Financial Options guide on <a href="https://www.financialoptions.pt/guias/comprar-casa-para-arrendar/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-terracotta-500 hover:text-terracotta-600 underline">buying a house to rent out</a> covers the mortgage implications. For the practical side of running a holiday rental &mdash; from guest communication to laundry logistics &mdash; drop us a line. We&rsquo;re happy to share what we&rsquo;ve learned.</p>

      <h2 class="mt-8 mb-3">Why Pombal? A quick reality check</h2>

      <p>Pombal isn&rsquo;t the Algarve. It doesn&rsquo;t have golden cliffs, English breakfasts on every corner, or a coastline of golf resorts. What it has is authenticity, affordability, and space.</p>

      <p>You&rsquo;re under two hours from Lisbon airport. You&rsquo;re 37 minutes from <a href="/beaches/" class="text-terracotta-500 hover:text-terracotta-600 underline">Praia da Leirosa</a>, a wide sandy beach that never gets packed. You&rsquo;re 50 minutes from Coimbra, one of Europe&rsquo;s oldest university cities. You can eat a three-course lunch with wine for &euro;12. And the neighbours still say bom dia when you walk past.</p>

      <p>That&rsquo;s what drew us here. It might be what draws you too. For more on why this corner of Portugal is worth your attention, see our <a href="/blog/discover-abiul-pombal-central-portugal/" class="text-terracotta-500 hover:text-terracotta-600 underline">guide to discovering Abiul and Pombal</a> and our <a href="/guide/pombal/" class="text-terracotta-500 hover:text-terracotta-600 underline">full Pombal area guide</a>.</p>

      <h2 class="mt-8 mb-3">The best first step? Come and stay.</h2>

      <p>No amount of scrolling on Idealista replaces being here. Walk the streets. Have a coffee in the square. Drive the country roads at golden hour. See if the rhythm of life in Central Portugal fits you.</p>

      <p><a href="/booking/" class="text-terracotta-500 hover:text-terracotta-600 underline">Book a stay at Casa dos Duques</a> and use it as your base while you explore. We&rsquo;re happy to share what we know &mdash; about agents, about banks, about which villages get the best light in winter. This article is the short version. The long version comes over a glass of wine by the pool.</p>
